		"brief": "An interactive demo titled \"Slanted coordinate grids\" teaching that a coordinate system in the plane needs only two NON-PARALLEL directions — perpendicularity is irrelevant.\n\nCANVAS: 2D plane centered on the origin, with a faint square grid for reference, axis ticks, and a subtle dark background (clean sans-serif labels, high contrast, no external libraries — single self-contained HTML file with inline canvas or SVG + JS).\n\nCORE OBJECTS:\n1. Two draggable direction vectors u and v drawn as arrows from the origin. Defaults u = (1,2), v = (3,1). Dragging either endpoint must update everything live.\n2. The parallelogram lattice generated by u and v: draw the two families of grid lines through the origin along u and along v (i.e. all lines {t*u + k*v} and {k*u + t*v} for integers k, real t), plus small dots at the integer lattice points {i*u + j*v}. The lattice must visibly slant as u and v are dragged.\n3. A draggable target point p, default (4,3). Solve p = a*u + b*v for real a,b. Draw the parallelogram path: origin -> a*u -> p -> b*v -> origin, with the two legs drawn in distinct colors and labeled \"a*u\" and \"b*v\". Display the numbers a and b.\n\nREADOUT PANEL showing:\n- u = (u_x, u_y), v = (v_x, v_y) with 2 decimals\n- det = u_x*v_y - u_y*v_x, and a, b\n- A status line that changes behavior:\n  * |det| comfortably nonzero: \"non-parallel directions: every point in the plane has exactly one recipe (a, b)\" (green)\n  * |det| small but nonzero (below about 0.3): \"nearly parallel — the recipes exist but the weights a, b are exploding\" (amber)\n  * |det| essentially zero: \"the directions lie on one line: a point on that line has infinitely many recipes, a point off it has none\" (red) — in this state, when the target is off the line, show a,b as undefined/dashes instead of numbers.\n\nPRESET BUTTONS (each animates or snaps the vectors):\n- \"axes\": u = (1,0), v = (0,1)  -> square grid\n- \"perpendicular, unequal length\": u = (2,0), v = (0,1)\n- \"slanted\": u = (1,2), v = (3,1)  -> parallelogram grid, recipe a = 1, b = 1 for p = (4,3)\n- \"parallel\": u = (1,2), v = (2,4) -> collapsed case\n\nREQUIREMENT: dragging u or v must never feel broken — clamp to canvas bounds, keep labels legible, and make sure the lattice and the a*u / b*v legs stay visually distinguishable (subtle transparency, line weights). The pedagogical point that must be obvious within ten seconds of dragging: the grid slants, yet the target still sits at exactly one corner.",
